A log call must not block the process making it.

syslog(3) sends to /var/run/syslog with a blocking send. macOS always has syslogd draining that
socket; here nothing does, so the datagram buffer fills and the send waits forever.

iTermServer froze on message 170, in the middle of logging a launch request's environment, and so
never forked the pty or started iTerm2's shell. Its last syscall in a 110 second run was a sendto
that never returned, while the rest of the container kept going. With MSG_DONTWAIT it finishes the
log, opens /dev/ptmx and execs login.

A dropped entry is what "no logging daemon" should cost, rather than the caller.

--- a/gen/oldsyslog.c
+++ b/gen/oldsyslog.c
@@ -242,7 +242,15 @@
 	/* Get connected, output the message to the local logger. */
 	if (!_sl_connected)
 		openlog(_sl_LogTag, _sl_LogStat | LOG_NDELAY, 0);
-	if (send(_sl_LogFile, tbuf, cnt, 0) >= 0)
+	/*
+	 * MSG_DONTWAIT, because a log call must never be able to stop the process making it.
+	 *
+	 * macOS always has syslogd draining /var/run/syslog. Here nothing does, so the datagram
+	 * buffer fills and a blocking send waits forever: iTermServer froze on message 170, in the
+	 * middle of logging a launch request's environment, and so never started iTerm2's shell.
+	 * A dropped entry is what "no logging daemon" should cost, rather than the caller.
+	 */
+	if (send(_sl_LogFile, tbuf, cnt, MSG_DONTWAIT) >= 0)
 		return;
 
 	/*
